==========================================================================
Prior work incorporated
==========================================================================

AetherProof, an open source attestation and receipt engine.
  Sole author: Pulkit Kr Srivastava.
  Licence: Apache-2.0 (v0.5.1).
  The public repository and package predate the publication of SIH26149.

Akhanda extends AetherProof. It is not a rename of it. Carried over are the
injective encoding discipline, the RFC 6962 Merkle domain separation, and the
split between verified and unverifiable reporting. New in Akhanda are dual party
signing, the physically separate witness node, external anchoring, and the
certificate generator written against the statutes.

See akhanda/CONTRIBUTORS.md for the per component record of authorship.

==========================================================================
Standards and third party material referenced
==========================================================================

NIST SP 800-88 Rev. 2, "Guidelines for Media Sanitization", a United States
Government publication and not subject to copyright in the United States.
Referenced for the Certificate of Sanitization structure and for the Clear,
Purge and Destroy vocabulary.

The Bharatiya Sakshya Adhiniyam, 2023 (Act 47 of 2023), Section 63(4) and the
Schedule. Text of Indian law, reproduced for compliance mapping.

RFC 6962 (Certificate Transparency) and RFC 8032 (EdDSA), IETF.

The NIST Computer Forensic Reference Data Set carving corpus is not committed to
this repository. Tests that require it skip with an explicit message rather than
passing silently. See akhanda/tests/test_recovery_nist.py for the download
commands.

==========================================================================
What this software does not claim
==========================================================================

The chain proves that nothing was altered, reordered or removed after it was
written. It cannot prove that no operation was withheld from the record
entirely. That is integrity, not completeness.

OpenTimestamps anchoring is a cryptographic guarantee. It is not, and is not
claimed to be, legal recognition under Indian law.

